tags: notice, stop notice, hibernate notice

A setting of the start of the service: the service reads the notices of the node on
the first virtio console of the guest. A notice tells the service that the node will
stop or hibernate the instance soon, so that the service can save its work.

The value is empty. Any other value is not valid.

The node writes one line for each notice to the console /dev/hvc0. A line is ASCII
text, 128 bytes or less, with a newline at its end: a verb, one space, and a unix time
in seconds. The verbs are STOP and HIBERNATE. Example: "STOP 1795713400". At that
time the node stops or hibernates the instance, also if the service is not ready. The
service ignores a line with a verb that it does not know. The node can write other
bytes to the console: the service ignores a line that does not have this form.

With no setting, the node sends no notice.
The node is bound: it sends the notice for each stop or hibernation that the instance
did not ask for. A node that cannot write to the console of the guest refuses the
service.

=== formal
channel=the first virtio console of the guest, at the path /dev/hvc0
class=setting
default=absent: the node sends no notice
line=ASCII; <verb> <space> <unix time in seconds as a base-10 integer> <newline>; at most 128 bytes with the newline
name=notice
place=service.container.init
time=the node stops or hibernates the instance at this time, whether the service is ready or not
value=empty
verb.unknown=the service ignores the line
verbs=HIBERNATE,STOP
when=before each stop and each hibernation that the instance did not ask for
